From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 07:56:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hwui: fall back to F_SEAL_WRITE where the kernel has no
 future-write seal

writeBlob seals the memfd it parcels a bitmap through with F_SEAL_GROW |
F_SEAL_SHRINK, plus F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE when the bitmap is immutable.
That seal arrived in Linux 5.1; this device runs 4.4, whose fcntl.h
knows only SEAL, SHRINK, GROW and WRITE. memfd_add_seals validates the
whole request against the seals it has and rejects all of it with
EINVAL, so every immutable bitmap big enough to take the ashmem path
fails to parcel.

It surfaces a long way from here. The one that found it was
Settings crashing on sign-in:

  UserSettings.assignDefaultPhoto -> UserManager.setUserIcon
  -> Bitmap.writeToParcel: java.lang.RuntimeException:
     Could not copy bitmap to parcel blob.

but nothing about it is specific to user icons: any immutable bitmap
over BLOB_MAX_INPLACE_LIMIT crossing a binder boundary hits it, and
smaller ones only escape by going in-place.

The comment already in the code is the licence for the fallback: it says
the two seals are functionally equivalent here because no writable
mappings have been made yet, and the pre-6.7 bug F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE
avoids cannot apply to a kernel older than the seal itself. So retry
with F_SEAL_WRITE, and only on EINVAL for an immutable bitmap.
---
 libs/hwui/jni/Bitmap.cpp | 29 ++++++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libs/hwui/jni/Bitmap.cpp b/libs/hwui/jni/Bitmap.cpp
index d97d1bca4616..b016ccc3dc13 100644
--- a/libs/hwui/jni/Bitmap.cpp
+++ b/libs/hwui/jni/Bitmap.cpp
@@ -807,17 +807,24 @@ static binder_status_t writeBlob(AParcel* parcel, uint64_t bitmapId, const SkBit
             return STATUS_NO_MEMORY;
         }
 
-        if (fcntl(fd, F_ADD_SEALS,
-                    // Disallow growing / shrinking.
-                    F_SEAL_GROW | F_SEAL_SHRINK
-                    // If immutable, disallow writing.
-                    // Use F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE instead of F_SEAL_WRITE to work around a bug in
-                    // pre-6.7 kernels.
-                    // There are no writable mappings made prior to this, so both seals are
-                    // functionally equivalent.
-                    // See: b/409846908#comment39
-                    | (immutable ? F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE : 0))) {
-            return STATUS_UNKNOWN_ERROR;
+        // Disallow growing / shrinking, and if immutable disallow writing.
+        // Use F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE instead of F_SEAL_WRITE to work around a bug in pre-6.7 kernels.
+        // There are no writable mappings made prior to this, so both seals are functionally
+        // equivalent.
+        // See: b/409846908#comment39
+        const int sealsBase = F_SEAL_GROW | F_SEAL_SHRINK;
+        if (fcntl(fd, F_ADD_SEALS, sealsBase | (immutable ? F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE : 0))) {
+            // F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE arrived in Linux 5.1. On an older kernel memfd_add_seals
+            // validates the whole request against the seals it knows and rejects all of it with
+            // EINVAL, so every immutable bitmap large enough to go through ashmem fails to
+            // parcel -- which surfaces far from here as "Could not copy bitmap to parcel blob".
+            // The comment above is the licence to fall back: with no writable mappings yet, plain
+            // F_SEAL_WRITE is equivalent, and the pre-6.7 bug it avoids cannot apply on a kernel
+            // that predates the seal itself.
+            if (!immutable || errno != EINVAL
+                    || fcntl(fd, F_ADD_SEALS, sealsBase | F_SEAL_WRITE)) {
+                return STATUS_UNKNOWN_ERROR;
+            }
         }
 
         // Workaround b/149851140 in AParcel_writeParcelFileDescriptor
